Subject: Read the frame's function object from the frame, not from a JSStackFrame the frame may not have

What this does
  Every generated step form began with
  `JSObject *p = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(rf->sf->cur_func);`. It now reads
  `rf->func`, the same function object, which every frame fill already stores
  (the record fill, the light fill and the entry record all set it).

Why we need it
  `rf->sf` is the frame's JSStackFrame, and a light frame deliberately has
  none: it is the frame a compiled call builds on its own C stack, and its
  `sf` is NULL by construction. Dereferencing `rf->sf->cur_func` therefore
  read through a null pointer the first time a compiled body ran an instruction
  as an op call on a light frame, which is every op the compiled body does not
  lower natively. `rf->func` names the same object, is already part of the
  light prefix, and is what the backtrace walk reads for the same reason.

  The three step forms that genuinely need the JSStackFrame -- `get_arg_el`,
  `make_closure`, `special_object`'s mapped-arguments case, the lexical and
  class ops, `put_arg_xfer` and the suspend paths -- keep their `rf->sf` use
  and are excluded from light frames by `regvm_code_light_safe`, so a frame
  that reaches them always has one.

What it adds
  Nothing: the same object, read from the frame instead of from a stack frame
  the light path does not have.

Bytecode version
  No.

